WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:06.650
Good day, I'm Irina Damljanović, and today I'll tell you about cell division — mitosis, cell division, mitosis.

2
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:12.830
So, there's another type of cell division, called meiosis, but we'll have a separate video about it.

3
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:19.550
This is a sort of ID card of mitosis, which doesn't tell you anything yet, but will by the end of this story.

4
00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:27.190
What is mitosis for? So that a lizard can regrow its tail. Cells divide, divide, divide, multiply, and that's how the lizard gets a new tail.

5
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:40.470
For a planarian, that flatworm, to split, that is, to carry out its asexual reproduction, it also divides its body, and then through mitosis, that is, cell division, makes up the missing parts.

6
00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:48.290
Something similar happens with the regeneration of the arms of a starfish or any other animal, that is, regeneration and restoration of what's missing.

7
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:54.890
And cell division, that is, the division of cells called mitosis, is responsible for all this.

8
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.070
Don't think this exists only in animals.

9
00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:03.920
Any healing of our wound is some mitotic divisions, but you don't need to wound yourself.

10
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.830
Mitosis is constantly present in skin cells, which renew heavily.

11
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:14.970
New cells are constantly created, and old ones die off and slough off the surface of the skin.

12
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:22.730
And finally, the most important thing: all of us arose from a single fertilized egg cell, but a single one.

13
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:34.170
And now we're very complex organisms, and a multitude of cells are responsible for all this, which divided, divided, divided and became more complex, and that's how we became who we are today.

14
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.290
I think that's quite enough to move on to what mitosis is.

15
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:47.250
From one cell two identical, exactly the same cells result, and the point is what goes into it and what is the end product.

16
00:01:47.910 --> 00:02:02.470
We have a separate video about what a cell is and what its parts are, but it's easy to imagine how the cytoplasm somehow divides in two, the organelles are distributed, some to one side, others to the other,

17
00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:08.950
and the cell somehow divides it, but what about the hereditary material, what about the DNA molecule,

18
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:17.650
DNA, parts of which are genes, and genes determine certain functions in the cell, and therefore in the organism,

19
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:25.210
how does that divide? Here we see the ability of DNA to pack itself very, very tightly and form a chromosome.

20
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.270
And here's a little pause, we'll come back to chromosomes.

21
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:37.090
Actually, let's continue. Humans have 46 chromosomes, but if you look closely, they're all paired.

22
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:45.110
Scientists arranged them by size and gave them numbers, so in fact it's 23 pairs of chromosomes.

23
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.790
From each pair one comes from the father and the other from the mother.

24
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:57.230
The number of chromosomes is constant and characteristic of the species.

25
00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.830
What does this mean? It means that one species always has the same specific number of chromosomes.

26
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:08.350
That is, all humans have 46 chromosomes, and potatoes have 48 chromosomes,

27
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.990
peas 14, fruit flies 8, and goldfish 94.

28
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:18.930
From this we clearly see that the complexity of an organism has nothing to do with the number of chromosomes,

29
00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.390
that is, with their count.

30
00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:25.690
And now let's return to mitosis. So, we know we have 46 chromosomes,

31
00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.950
and from one cell through mitosis we need to get two identical ones.

32
00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.370
That is, with 46 chromosomes: how do you get 46 from 46?

33
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.250
This is where chromatids play a role.

34
00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:39.410
Here is one chromosome, that is, a certain number of DNA molecules,

35
00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.190
which has packed itself very tightly, like when you take aluminum foil

36
00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.390
and then crumple it.

37
00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.050
It packed itself and doubled.

38
00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.290
This is a chromosome.

39
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.330
It doubled in such a way that it copied everything exactly from this one side to the other

40
00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:00.570
and so formed a chromosome consisting of two sister chromatids.

41
00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:05.490
That is, each half is a chromatid, joined by a centromere, where there is no genetic material.

42
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.410
It's just a structure that holds these two chromatids together.

43
00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.290
That is, this is a chromosome, and this is a chromosome.

44
00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:18.130
At the same time when we see such an X-shaped chromosome, it's clear to us that the cell is preparing to divide.

45
00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.720
And how does a cell live?

46
00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:30.890
We find this out through the cell cycle, that is, the cell cycle, where one human cell — now we'll be dealing with human cells, they'll be our example.

47
00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:40.290
It begins its life emerging from division: some other cell divided, and so we got one, let's call it a baby cell.

48
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.730
And what must it do? Like any baby, it must grow.

49
00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.790
Growth — that's why the letter G is here, this green phase.

50
00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.930
In general this whole circle represents the life of one cell,

51
00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.350
which begins with division, then continues with growth,

52
00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.490
and then in a longer period

53
00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.790
the DNA doubles, then the cell continues to grow

54
00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.510
and only then is ready for a new division, that is, for its death,

55
00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.690
and to give new life, that is, two new cells.

56
00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:13.100
That is, from division to division lasts the life of one cell.

57
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:30.190
True, there are some specialized cells, nerve, muscle — meaning the body's muscles, large ones — and heart muscle cells, which somewhere stop their life — not stop life, but stop development, halt the cell cycle at this phase.

58
00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.030
And that's why there's this purple arrow here that goes around in a circle.

59
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:39.570
They're in this phase, they've divided, become super-specialists in what they do.

60
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.170
And so they stay, they don't divide further.

61
00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.510
And now we're ready to get to know mitosis in detail.

62
00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:56.470
Interphase, that is, this drawing, represents everything in the circle of the cell cycle that is not division.

63
00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:12.630
That is, here we have the hereditary material, the nucleus, and here, in preparation for division, two special organelles have formed, so-called centrosomes, which I didn't mention in the talk about the cell, because their role is mainly in cell division.

64
00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:26.970
Now we have two of them, and each moves to the poles of the cell, that is, to opposite sides. At this time the nuclear envelope slowly breaks down, disappears, and we increasingly distinguish chromosomes.

65
00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:34.970
That is, chromosomes did not arise from nothing, but are that very hereditary material, that is, DNA, which packs more and more tightly into these chromosomes.

66
00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:36.950
Here they are, the centrosomes.

67
00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:56.110
That is, here you see kind of a cylinder and another cylinder, and these are actually some filaments, that is, fibers, like those of the cytoskeleton, like that cell scaffolding that forms a network along which the chromosomes will be positioned.

68
00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.310
Here's what this network looks like.

69
00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:10.930
The chromosomes line up exactly in the middle, and then, hooked onto these fibers, split into chromatids, and each chromatid approaches its side of the cell.

70
00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:18.670
And then a nucleus slowly forms around each newly formed hereditary material.

71
00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:25.990
The cytoplasm also pinches in at this central place, and we get two new cells.

72
00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:32.090
This is all one process, we could show it like a film.

73
00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:39.830
However, to make it easier to study the whole story, scientists divided it into phases.

74
00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:49.890
So, that whole stage of preparing for division is called interphase.

75
00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.630
When there are visible chromosomes, it's called prophase,

76
00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.310
when these centrosomes begin to move to their places.

77
00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:00.130
When the centrosomes are already in their places, and the chromosomes in the middle — that's metaphase,

78
00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.890
and colleagues teaching in English: metaphase and middle can be joined as M and M.

79
00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.550
Anaphase — when the chromatids separate and move to the poles,

80
00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:13.550
and telophase — "telos" usually denotes something located at the end,

81
00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.290
where new nuclei are now forming,

82
00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.370
and cytokinesis is included here, cytokinesis relates to the cytoplasm,

83
00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.470
that is, to the division of the cytoplasm,

84
00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:29.150
and so we get complete division, that is, pro-, meta-, ana-, telophase, P, M, A, T,

85
00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.430
or in the cell cycle, that is, in the life of the cell,

86
00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:38.170
this is only this short part, if we consider the whole life to be the whole circle, the full circle.

87
00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:46.890
So as not to stop only at diagrams, let's show how this looks in microscope images.

88
00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:53.210
That is, these are photographs, or rather micrographs, and these are specially processed contrasts, so

89
00:08:53.210 --> 00:09:03.490
parts of the cell are stained so that only the cytoskeleton fibers are visible, that is, from these centrosomes, and the chromosomes they pull.

90
00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.250
So, first comes interphase, chromosomes aren't visible.

91
00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:15.380
Prophase: chromosomes are already forming, and here we see the centrosomes moving to their places.

92
00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.360
Metaphase — we already have it here too.

93
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.670
The chromosomes in the middle.

94
00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.790
In anaphase they separate.

95
00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:27.210
Here we see how they've begun to separate,

96
00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:39.610
and in telophase they're already very close to the poles of the cell, and the cytoplasm slowly begins to divide too.

97
00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:47.990
So, cell division, mitosis, its equation could be 46 equals 46 plus 46 in the case of humans.

98
00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:56.290
That is, mathematically a complete illogicality and impossibility, yet in biology it's possible, because one parent cell

99
00:09:56.290 --> 00:10:02.090
gives two completely identical daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes

100
00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:08.290
and with the same chromosomes, and we saw that this is possible precisely thanks to

101
00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:12.110
that chromosome trick — making two chromatids out of itself.

102
00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:19.450
That is, chromosomes duplicate, chromatids separate to the poles

103
00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.770
and these chromatids become new cells.

104
00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:26.170
Summa summarum: mitosis produces new identical cells.